//! check: run(exit=trap(assert))
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.trap.map, std.bytes
//!
//! `bytes.slice` with an out-of-order range traps `assert`, and the KIND is
//! a recorded delta the module's doc explains: the reviewed contract said
//! `bounds`, which is what a `Bytes` type with slicing syntax would
//! inherit from the language — but the interim currency is `List[int]`,
//! there is no slice syntax to fault, so the check is std's own and
//! `assert` is the kind std can raise.
//!
//! It is held as a test rather than left to the doc because a range check
//! that silently returned an empty list would be the exact failure §2's
//! trap rule exists to prevent: a caller's arithmetic mistake answered with
//! plausible data.

use std.bytes

fn main() -> !int {
    let b = bytes.from_str("wolf")
    let backwards = bytes.slice(b, 3, 1)
    if bytes.is_empty(backwards) {} else { return 1 }
    0
}
